it's football's biggest kept secret it's  a cycle of  toxic masculinity because as an adult  once you've been through it you want to  bury it as much as you can  for years after he was released from  fulham academy max couldn't bear to  watch  football his body was so wrecked a kick  about with friends was a distant dream  he had been among the country's most  talented young players  as a youth international for wales he  starred alongside gareth bale and aaron  ramsey  max noble one of the english-born  players in the side  for the thousands of boys who don't make  it those  sold a dream and cast adrift with little  regard for what happens next  it is he says a form of child abuse  there's no aftercare for young boys that  have given and dedicated their whole  lives  so sometimes one football club maybe a  couple of football clubs but essentially  academy systems  and for you to then feel like a failure  after is what  why we see boys committing suicide now  why we see boys self-harming i've got  two friends that tried to kill  themselves i've got another two  that actually hung themselves these are  boys who went to  football academies yeah different clubs  i think just three different clubs in  south london  so it's a massive a massive issue in  football  max was signed by fulham aged 15  encouraged by the club to quit school  before taking his gcses  by 19 he'd been released requiring  double knee surgery  which he believes was caused by medical  treatment at the club  at 16 i'm getting injections in my knees  at 16 years old  getting painkillers given to me daily  i've got stories of my friends who would  go out with four or five strappings on  their legs just to train  how many injections you had in your  knees so many so many  i mean 10 15 over the course of a couple  of years  max also says he experienced racism at  the club  we were told that we had bad attitudes  which i think is another massive issue  in football is that how all young black  boys are labeled with bad attitudes  um so there was seven or eight black  boys in the team  we're all from different parts of london  all from different backgrounds  completely different families  and we were told that because we've got  bad attitudes and we don't listen and we  don't want to learn  that we can't eat in the canteen so we  can't eat the food in the canteen we  have to eat in the changing room  so they gave us sandwiches afterwards  shower in a dirty chain room they put  the sandwiches on a tray on the floor  in a statement fulham football club said  we condemn bullying racism and  discrimination in any form  and work hard to ensure they have no  place here the club is investigating and  today has found nothing to support the  historic claims  the club will liaise with all relevant  parties as the process continues  max is now campaigning for a change in  the law to make it obligatory for clubs  to look after young players who are  released  heartbroken with no qualifications and  no idea what to do next  those he says are football's forgotten  boys and they're everywhere  martha kellner sky news
 

